# Study: hook_output_preview (B30) by itself, against no Trove. Written 2026-11-25, before any run.
# This is a TEMPLATE, not a registered study. Do not run it as it is.
#
# Why it exists: hook_output_preview replaces a long tool output (Bash, PowerShell, Grep text, MCP) with a preview of
# at most 2,000 bytes and keeps the whole output fetchable with trove_fetch_full. A shadow replay of 177 local Claude
# Code sessions (one developer, 2026-11-25) estimated it would act on about 1 in 85 tool results and keep about 1.6 MB
# out of context before any fetch-back. That says nothing about whether the agent then needs the text back, which costs
# an extra turn and the whole output anyway. Only this comparison can say whether it saves money.
#
# Two runs, the same design, different tasks:
#   preview-1a  tasks/largeout       4 tasks written for this: three where the preview keeps what the agent needs (an
#                                    error line in a run log, a warning in a verbose build, a long search) and one
#                                    adversarial task where the needed line sits unflagged in the middle of a listing,
#                                    so the agent has to fetch it back or search again.
#   preview-1b  tasks/longsession30  the existing long-session suite, to see what it does on ordinary work, where long
#                                    non-file output is rare (launch-3's plugin arm saw no output-shortening at all).
#
# To use it (the benchmark runbook, Part D):
#   1. Copy it to bench/studies/preview-1a.yaml (and preview-1b.yaml with `tasks: tasks/longsession30`, repeats: 2,
#      id: preview-1b), and replace every <PLACEHOLDER>. <NEW_SEED> is a fresh number, used for both seeds.
#      <FROM_PILOT> comes from a pilot, as for the other studies.
#   2. Run the hook probe on the VM's Claude Code first; the preview stays in shadow for any tool it has not seen
#      honour updatedToolOutput:  trove hooks probe --live --max-usd 0.35 --json > /tmp/hookprobe.json
#   3. python -m trovebench plan   --study bench/studies/preview-1a.yaml --hook-capabilities /tmp/hookprobe.json
#   4. python -m trovebench prereg bench/studies/preview-1a.yaml      (locks it; commit the lock BEFORE running)
#
# The baseline is `direct` (no Trove, no gateway). The preview condition registers Trove's MCP server, as a user who
# turns it on must, so its 351 tokens of tool definitions per request are paid in that arm (PRINCIPLES.md rule 15).
#
# What to expect, stated before the run: on largeout the preview acts on every task; whether it pays depends on how
# often the agent fetches back (the adversarial task should). On longsession30 it may act in few runs; then "no clear
# difference" means it had little to do, and the MCP definitions alone may make it cost more. Read
#   python bench/tools/hook_activity.py <results>
# before the table, and report the result as it is, including a loss.

id: preview-1a
title: "hook_output_preview by itself, against no Trove, on tasks with long output"
hypothesis: >
  Turning on hook_output_preview by itself lowers the cost per task against no Trove on tasks whose workflow prints
  long, non-file output, without lowering the share of tasks that pass.
primary_metric: billed_cost_usd
secondary_metrics: [success_rate, latency_s]
baseline_condition: direct
conditions: [direct, alone_hook_output_preview]
tasks: tasks/largeout
repeats: 10
order_seed: <NEW_SEED>
cost_basis: agent
analysis:
  alpha: 0.05
  bootstrap_resamples: 10000
  bootstrap_seed: <NEW_SEED>
  multiple_comparison: holm
ship_rule:
  min_cost_reduction_pct: 5
  success_margin_pts: 5
  min_pairs: 30
budget:
  batch_cap_usd: <FROM_PILOT>
  run_cap_usd: <FROM_PILOT>
